{"author":{"id":"1d5f412cdeafe9987ad9876b0995bf5032718543d34c038a50cdb41808e9874c"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788305204,"nonce":"fbp4I6RBCYk1pD9Eyxu/ufjt0ao=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOULVc1M8AAAABPPiGqA","github-url":"https://github.com/kanya-approve/torram-ng/issues/21","origin":"github"},"title":"Verification loop is O(files x candidates x pieces x files) and does not scale","message":"This is the dominant performance problem and it is algorithmic, not constant-factor.\n\nFor every torrent file, for every candidate of that size, `guess_file()` rewinds the piece stream and iterates **every piece in the entire torrent**, discarding the ones that do not belong to the current file:\n\n```python\npieces.seek(0)\nwhile True:\n    hash = pieces.read(20)\n    if not hash: break\n    idx, file_offset = get_chunk(files_sizes_array, offset * piece_length)   # O(F) linear scan\n    if idx == file_idx:\n        ...\n    offset += 1\n```\n\n`get_chunk()` is itself a linear scan over the file list, so the cost is roughly\n\n```\ncandidates x pieces x (files / 2)\n```\n\n**1 TB torrent, 1 MiB pieces, 1000 files, 2 candidates each:** about 1e12 interpreter steps, on the order of 12 hours of pure bookkeeping — to support roughly 14 minutes of actual SHA-1 work. Measured SHA-1 throughput on a modern x86 core with SHA-NI is about 2.36 GB/s.\n\n**100 GB torrent, 50,000 files:** about 1e14 steps. It does not finish.\n\nBoth numbers are before a single byte is read from disk.\n\nFixes, in order of impact:\n- Iterate only the pieces that overlap the current file: `first = start // piece_length`, `last = (start + length - 1) // piece_length`. Removes the outer redundancy entirely.\n- Replace the linear `get_chunk()` scan with `bisect` over a precomputed cumulative-offset array, or walk the file index monotonically since pieces are visited in order.\n\nTogether these turn the hot loop into O(pieces) with O(1) mapping.","files":null}]}